1f49c7fd (3133 1792861145630 "guenchi" (("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f335") (src . "test: one bounded wait on a child's trace, loaded by the fixtures that wait\n\ncli1.sc waited on a child with five shell spins (four of 4000 iterations\naround a grep for a trace word, one of 8000 for a file to have content),\nthe instrument retire-race.sc had just replaced: a spin measures the\nmachine, not the time, and on a slow runner the fixture went on before\nthe child had reached its point.\n\n- New test/trace-poll.ss, loaded by retire-race.sc and cli1.sc the way the\n  other loaded helpers are: `trace-wait-text` (words file bound) returns\n  shell text that sets `w` to `yes` when the file holds any of the words,\n  or, with no words, has content, and to `no` only from a look taken after\n  the bound on CLOCK_MONOTONIC; one perl process, a look every 50 ms, its\n  own alarm at bound + 10 s. `trace-poll` adds the report line retire-race\n  writes; `file-wait-text` is the no-words form. A quote in an argument or\n  a bound that is not an exact whole number is refused when the text is\n  built. Named `.ss` so the runner never takes it for a fixture.\n- cli1.sc: the five waits go through the helper with the fixture's bound;\n  a CONTROL for the file-wait form (an empty file reads `no` after the\n  bound and before 10 s, a file with content reads `yes` at once).\n- retire-race.sc: the procedure moved out; its CONTROL pins the helper's\n  bound and alarm for every caller.\n- test/RUN.md names the helper in the delivery list; test/rows-baseline.txt:\n  cli1 100 124, retire-race 19 23.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 470))))
